# Backend Engineering Standards

## Architecture & Layering
- Follow Clean Architecture / Layered Architecture: strictly separate Routing, Controllers, Services (Domain Logic), and Repositories (Data Access).
- Keep controllers thin: handle only request validation, HTTP status mapping, and delegation to domain services.
- Never write raw business or calculation logic inside HTTP controllers, CLI commands, or route closures.

## API Contracts & RESTful Design
- Adhere to RESTful conventions: use semantic HTTP verbs (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`/`PATCH`, `DELETE`) and explicit HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500).
- Standardize all JSON API responses with consistent envelopes: `{ success: boolean, message: string, data?: any, errors?: any }` or RFC 7807 Problem Details.
- Enforce pagination on all collection endpoints. Never return unbounded lists.
- Support idempotency on payment, ordering, and state-mutating endpoints using Idempotency Keys.

## Defensive Validation & Error Handling
- Validate all incoming request payloads against strict server-side schema rules. Never rely on frontend validation alone.
- Never swallow exceptions with empty `catch` blocks. Log error details with contextual parameters and return clean, sanitized messages to the client.
- Wrap multi-table mutation operations inside database transactions with explicit rollback on error.
- Enforce explicit timeouts and circuit breaking on all external third-party API dependencies.
